  "worked_solution": "The function $F(t)=f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable over the common domain where both $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. A function is differentiable if and only if each component is differentiable.\n\nLet's now find the intervals where each of the functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable.\n\nFor the function $f(t):$ \n\n- The first component $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(t+1)$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The second component ${4}^{t}$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component $\\sqrt{2-t}$ is differentiable on $(-\\infty ,2).$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $f(t)$ is $(-\\infty ,2).$ \n\nFor the function $g(t):$ \n\n- The first component $t-2$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The second component $\\sqrt{t+1}$ is differentiable on $(-1,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component ${t}^{3}$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $g(t)$ is $(-1,\\infty ).$\n\nFinally, the common domain of differentiability for both functions is \n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,2)\\cap (-1,\\infty )=(-1,2).\n$$",
